// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// wisp32-watch
// Jose AVILES
//
// PLATFORM: Waveshare ESP32-S3-Touch-AMOLED-2.06
// Display:  CO5300 QSPI AMOLED, 410x502, via Arduino_GFX_Library
// SD:       SD_MMC (native ESP32 SDMMC peripheral, 1-bit mode)
// Touch:    FT3168 cap-touch — double-tap sleep/wake, plus tap and swipe
//           gestures across the on-device pages (see loop()'s gesture dispatch)
// Arduino IDE: ESP32S3 Dev Module | OPI PSRAM | 16MB Flash
//              USB CDC On Boot: Enabled | 16M with spiffs
//
// Ported from waveshare_badge28_touch_only.ino (Waveshare ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-2.8).
// KEPT: minimal clock screen (time+date), entire web server
//       (file/knowledge/image/chat-log management + AI assistant endpoints),
//       all SD/encryption helper functions, WiFi + NTP setup.
// DROPPED (at initial port — see NOTE below): main menu, all touch handling,
//       on-device popup pages (weather, expense tracker, timezone, mood chips,
//       todo/reminders on-device UI, assistant/chat on-device UI, settings
//       page, keyboard UI, sleep/wake logic, showWebServerMode()/
//       drawWebServerStats() on-device screen), LGFX/ST7789/CST328 driver
//       code, U8g2/VLW font system, audio/mic/speaker/mp3 recording code.
// NOTE: much of that DROPPED list was later re-added and is live in this
//       build — touch handling, sleep/wake, and the on-device Settings, AI
//       Assistant, Translator, Voice Notes and QR-code pages. The list above
//       describes the initial port, not the current feature set.
//
// Pin assignments (Waveshare ESP32-S3-Touch-AMOLED-2.06):
//   LCD (QSPI):  SDIO0=4 SDIO1=5 SDIO2=6 SDIO3=7 SCLK=11 CS=12 RESET=8
//   SD (SDMMC 1-bit): CLK=2 CMD=1 DATA=3
//
// ── MEMORY RULE — READ BEFORE ADDING ANY NEW GLOBAL/STATIC VARIABLE ──
// This board's internal SRAM is small and PERMANENTLY shared with the live
// AI Assistant's WebSocket (its TLS session alone costs ~50-60KB for as
// long as it's open) and every tool-call REST connection (web_search,
// ask_file_question, etc.) that has to open a second TLS connection
// alongside it — those two together already leave only ~10-30KB of
// contiguous room, barely enough for one handshake. PSRAM, by contrast, is
// abundant (multiple free MB, see ESP.getFreePsram()) and completely
// separate.
//
// A plain global/static array — `Type name[N];`, `String name[N];`, any
// fixed-size struct-of-Strings array — is placed by the LINKER into
// internal SRAM, unconditionally, for the device's entire runtime, whether
// or not that data is ever actually used. This has ALREADY broken live AI
// connectivity outright at least twice (confirmed live both times): once
// when the Tasks feature's activePageTasks[] added ~1,500 String objects
// to internal SRAM, and again from the cumulative growth of several
// smaller globals (activeToolDescs[]'s tool-description text alone grew
// ~6KB) after the first fix. Both were fixed the same way — see below.
//
// THE RULE: any array of Strings, or any fixed-size array of a struct
// containing Strings, MUST be PSRAM-backed — never a plain global array.
// Follow the exact pattern already used throughout this file: declare it
// as a pointer (`Type* name = nullptr;`), allocate it once via
// `ps_malloc()` + placement-new in its own `allocXInPsram()` function
// (see allocPageTasksInPsram(), allocToolDescsInPsram(),
// allocContactsInPsram(), allocWifiNetworksInPsram(),
// allocAiToolHistoryInPsram(), allocColorThemesInPsram(),
// allocQrEntriesInPsram(), allocAiTextLinesInPsram(),
// allocPendingEmailAttachmentsInPsram() for
// working examples), call that allocator early in setup() (grouped
// together, search for "allocPageTasksInPsram()" to find them), and guard
// every write site so a (very unlikely) allocation failure degrades that
// one feature gracefully instead of crashing — never leave a null pointer
// reachable from a write. A handful of small, short-lived, few-entries
// String arrays (e.g. the various aiXModels[] model-name lists) aren't
// worth the added complexity — use judgment on genuinely trivial cases,
// but default to PSRAM for anything else. This is the one recurring
// mistake in this codebase's history that has repeatedly, silently broken
// a feature nothing about the change looked related to.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

